    <!-- 1. DATA TYPES (Blue) -->
    <section id="data-types" class="card theme-blue">
        <div class="card-header">
            <i class="fa-solid fa-layer-group"></i> Data Types & Variables
        </div>
        <div class="card-body">
            <p class="mb-4"><strong>Variables</strong> are containers for storing data values. In Python, variables are dynamically typed (you don't declare the type explicitly).</p>

            <h3>Core Data Types</h3>
            <div class="table-responsive mb-4">
                <table>
                    <thead>
                        <tr>
                            <th>Type</th>
                            <th>Description</th>
                            <th>Examples</th>
                        </tr>
                    </thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr>
                            <td><span class="badge">int</span></td>
                            <td>Whole numbers</td>
                            <td><code>10</code>, <code>-5</code></td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><span class="badge">float</span></td>
                            <td>Decimal numbers</td>
                            <td><code>3.14</code>, <code>2.0</code></td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><span class="badge">str</span></td>
                            <td>Text strings. (Python has no separate 'char' type)</td>
                            <td><code>"Hello"</code>, <code>'A'</code></td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><span class="badge">bool</span></td>
                            <td>Logic values</td>
                            <td><code>True</code>, <code>False</code></td>
                        </tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>

            <h3>Variable Swapping (The "Cup" Analogy)</h3>
            <p>To swap two variables <code>a</code> and <code>b</code>, you need a temporary variable (like a third cup).</p>

            <pre><code class="python">a = 5      # Cup A (Water)
b = 10     # Cup B (Tea)

temp = a   # 1. Pour 'a' into empty temp cup
a = b      # 2. Pour 'b' into 'a'
b = temp   # 3. Pour 'temp' into 'b'</code></pre>

            <div class="tip-box">
                <span class="tip-title"><i class="fa-solid fa-lightbulb"></i> Revision Tips</span>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Misconception:</strong> Don't confuse assignment (<code>=</code>) with equality comparison (<code>==</code>).</li>
                    <li><strong>Exam Tip:</strong> If asked to "declare" a variable in pseudocode, Python just assigns a value (e.g., <code>score = 0</code>).</li>
                    <li><strong>Watch Out:</strong> <code>"5"</code> is a String, <code>5</code> is an Integer. You cannot add them directly!</li>
                </ul>
            </div>
        </div>
    </section>

    <!-- 2. OPERATORS (Green) -->
    <section id="operators" class="card theme-green">
        <div class="card-header">
            <i class="fa-solid fa-calculator"></i> Operators
        </div>
        <div class="card-body">
            <p class="mb-4">Operators perform operations on variables and values. Python has specific symbols for arithmetic, comparison, and logic.</p>

            <h3>Arithmetic Operators</h3>
            <div class="table-responsive mb-4">
                <table>
                    <thead>
                        <tr>
                            <th>Operator</th>
                            <th>Description</th>
                            <th>Example</th>
                        </tr>
                    </thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>+</code></td>
                            <td>Add</td>
                            <td><code>5 + 2</code> &rarr; <code>7</code></td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>-</code></td>
                            <td>Subtract</td>
                            <td><code>5 - 2</code> &rarr; <code>3</code></td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>*</code></td>
                            <td>Multiply</td>
                            <td><code>5 * 2</code> &rarr; <code>10</code></td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>/</code></td>
                            <td>Float Division (Decimal result)</td>
                            <td><code>7 / 2</code> &rarr; <code>3.5</code></td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>//</code></td>
                            <td>Floor Division (Integer result)</td>
                            <td><code>7 // 2</code> &rarr; <code>3</code></td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>%</code></td>
                            <td>Modulus (Remainder)</td>
                            <td><code>7 % 2</code> &rarr; <code>1</code></td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>**</code></td>
                            <td>Exponentiation (Power)</td>
                            <td><code>2 ** 3</code> &rarr; <code>8</code></td>
                        </tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>

            <div class="grid-2">
                <div>
                    <h3>Relational Operators</h3>
                    <div class="table-responsive">
                        <table>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><code>==</code></td>
                                    <td>Equal to</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><code>!=</code></td>
                                    <td>Not equal to</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><code>&lt;</code></td>
                                    <td>Less than</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><code>&gt;</code></td>
                                    <td>Greater than</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><code>&lt;=</code></td>
                                    <td>Less or Equal</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><code>&gt;=</code></td>
                                    <td>Greater or Equal</td>
                                </tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <div>
                    <h3>Logical Operators</h3>
                    <div class="table-responsive">
                        <table>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><code>and</code></td>
                                    <td>Both must be true</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><code>or</code></td>
                                    <td>At least one is true</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><code>not</code></td>
                                    <td>Invert value (True &rarr; False)</td>
                                </tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="tip-box">
                <span class="tip-title"><i class="fa-solid fa-lightbulb"></i> Critical Differences</span>
                <ul>
                    <li><code>/</code> <em>always</em> creates a float. <code>//</code> <em>always</em> rounds down to an int.</li>
                    <li><strong>Short-circuiting:</strong> <code>or</code> stops early if the first condition is True.</li>
                    <li><strong>BODMAS/BIDMAS</strong> applies to programming too!</li>
                </ul>
            </div>
        </div>
    </section>

    <!-- 3. STRING MANIPULATION (Purple) -->
    <section id="strings" class="card theme-purple">
        <div class="card-header">
            <i class="fa-solid fa-font"></i> String Manipulation
        </div>
        <div class="card-body">
            <p class="mb-4">Strings are sequences of characters. They are <strong>immutable</strong> (cannot be changed in place).</p>

            <h3>Slicing Syntax: <code>string[start : stop : step]</code></h3>
            <pre><code class="python">txt = "Computer"
# Indices: 01234567

print(txt[0:3])   # "Com" (Indices 0, 1, 2 - STOP excluded)
print(txt[3:])    # "puter" (From 3 to end)
print(txt[-1])    # "r" (Last character)</code></pre>

            <h3>Common Methods</h3>
            <div class="table-responsive">
                <table>
                    <tr>
                        <td><code>len(s)</code></td>
                        <td>Returns number of characters (including spaces).</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td><code>s.strip()</code></td>
                        <td>Removes whitespace from start and end.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td><code>s.replace(old, new)</code></td>
                        <td>Replaces all occurrences of a substring.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td><code>s + s</code></td>
                        <td>Concatenation (joining strings).</td>
                    </tr>
                </table>
            </div>

            <div class="tip-box">
                <span class="tip-title"><i class="fa-solid fa-lightbulb"></i> Note</span>
                <p>Slicing <code>[a:b]</code> includes <code>a</code> but <strong>excludes</strong> <code>b</code>. Also, string methods return a <em>new</em> string; they don't modify the original.</p>
            </div>
        </div>
    </section>

    <!-- 4. ERROR HANDLING (Teal) -->
    <section id="errors" class="card theme-teal">
        <div class="card-header">
            <i class="fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation"></i> Error Handling
        </div>
        <div class="card-body">
            <p class="mb-4">Exception handling prevents programs from crashing when runtime errors occur (like dividing by zero).</p>

            <pre><code class="python">try:
    num = int(input("Enter number: "))
    result = 100 / num
except ValueError:
    # Runs if user types text instead of a number
    print("That is not a valid number!")
except ZeroDivisionError:
    # Runs if user types 0
    print("Cannot divide by zero!")
except Exception as e:
    # Generic catch-all
    print(f"Unknown error: {e}")
finally:
    # ALWAYS runs (for cleanup)
    print("Execution finished.")</code></pre>

            <div class="tip-box">
                <span class="tip-title"><i class="fa-solid fa-lightbulb"></i> Tips</span>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Syntax Errors</strong> happen before code runs. <strong>Exceptions</strong> happen <em>during</em> execution.</li>
                    <li>Always catch specific errors (e.g. <code>ValueError</code>) before generic ones.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>
        </div>
    </section>

